WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
There’s this scene in the movie Flashdance,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.160
where some b-boys are toprocking, busting out backspins, freezes,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.880
and a move that would later be called the moonwalk.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.640
But they aren’t just some random kids;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.620
they’re members of the Rock Steady Crew —

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.040
one of the most influential [dance] crews that brought Hollywood fame to breaking.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.260
Many people recognize the term breakdancing,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:33.160
a name coined by the media in the 1980s, but the pioneers and practitioners of the dance form call it b-boying or breaking.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:39.180
The name breaking is broadly believed to have come from DJ Kool Herc in the Bronx in New York City.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.780
When hosting block parties in the 1970s, he started isolating the beats to create breaks in the songs.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:51.000
Joseph: It refers to a moment in the song where you build tension by having the

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.000
instruments drop out and just having percussion or percussion and bass. Basically just the rhythm section.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.560
A really important scholar, Barbara Browning, had this great way to describe it where she says,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.380
“You feel compelled to fill the silence with motion.”

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:08.020
The break is a part of a lot of African American and Afro Caribbean music,

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.660
especially Latino music is a big part of the roots of this dance.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.620
Puerto Rican and Cuban music in particular.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.960
Traditionally, b-boys and b-girls danced to a mix of funk, soul, jazz, techno, rock, and disco.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:35.200
Joseph: The sound of the music is very aggressive, and very percussive. It’s a battle dance, so the music get people in the mood to battle.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.800
Joseph: You want to really be able to rock the beat, you want to be able to play with those rhythms, and reflect them in your body.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:48.040
Breaking is made up of four elements: toprock, downrock, power moves, and freezes.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.620
Toprock is the foot movement from a standing position.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.440
It’s the first display of style and a warm-up for the acrobatics of downrock.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.420
Downrock is when breakers use their hands and feet on the floor.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.860
This is where they show off foot speed and control of their footwork.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:11.460
From there a b-boy can move into power moves like the windmill and headspins.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.240
These moves need momentum and a ton of physical power.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.100
And the last element is a freeze,

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:23.600
when a b-girl stops dancing and holds a position that requires incredible balance.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:30.940
These elements often draw movements from disciplines like kung-fu, tap dance, gymnastics, and capoeira.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:39.560
But there’s more to it. When Breaking first began, it was a way for disadvantaged kids in the Bronx to be recognized.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:48.820
It helped transform their street culture -- where gang violence slowly made way for neighborhood crews that used dance to compete against each other.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.420
Now, with those competitions being commercialized,

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.700
breaking means something different for the newer generation of b-boys and b-girls.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.120
Miguel: Competitions for me with breaking is a huge asset.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:05.660
I started competing because, the energy of the battle is like, amazing, plus it was money to win.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.440
I wanted to provide for my mom, my family, and help them out. You know?

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.760
It’s a way for me to just prove myself, and put myself out there, and get better.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.520
I feel like I get better at competitions.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.100
But there those who believe that mainstream breaking has left some traditions behind.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:29.820
Chief 69: A lot of people don’t have that original style no more.The original b-boys would go to parties more—they would do more party rocking.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.000
They would interact more like on a personal level with the crowd, with the fly-girls.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.480
The b-boys today, they just go to competitions.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.520
They don't necessarily dance at parties.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.360
Joseph: People are attracted to the acrobatic aspects of it, people are attracted to the flamboyance aspect of it.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.760
But bottomline of it it's a dance and all that the gymnastics, the acrobatics, are all flavoring

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:02.360
to add on top of the core which is really dancing, which is rhythm and movement and self expression.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.760
Breaking in its original form is more than just the dance.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:15.880
It’s a connection to your family, your friends, your neighborhood rivals—your community.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.080
Chief 69: In hip hop culture it’s very important to be connected to the community. Because we come from these communities.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:29.240
If we can’t even attempt to give back in some type of way, culturally, financially, economically, spiritually, philosophically --

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.380
I feel we fall short.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:36.500
Joseph: A lot about hip hop dance and hip hop in general is getting people to notice you.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:43.400
I think that’s a personal thing for each individual person, but it’s also a political statement.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.320
All of that stuff is embedded in the dance.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:51.520
Just to say, not only am I important as an individual, but community is important, and the concerns of my community are important,

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:54.840
and the perspective, the point of view of my community is important.

